   "source": [
    "## 1. Business Understanding\n",
    "Define the business problem, stakeholder/user, decision, prediction/decision moment, scope and measurable success criteria. Critically assess scenario assumptions. Do not invent company facts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e7add752",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Business problem.** The grocery loyalty programme sends campaigns (email, SMS or push, with 0–15% discount) to its members. Contacts cost money and customer attention, so not everyone can be contacted. The question is: **which loyalty members should receive the next campaign so that the limited contacts reach customers who are likely to buy?**\n",
    "\n",
    "| Element | Definition |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| Stakeholder / user | CRM / loyalty campaign manager (decides the send list) |\n",
    "| Decision | Include or exclude a customer from the contact list of the next campaign |\n",
    "| Prediction moment | After channel and discount are chosen, **before** the campaign is sent. Only information known at that moment may be used |\n",
    "| Target | `conversion_flag` (1 = customer bought after the campaign) |\n",
    "| Scope | Ranking existing loyalty members within one campaign. Not in scope: choosing discount or channel, new customers |\n",
    "| Capacity | 12% of the customers can be contacted per campaign (**scenario assumption**) |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Measurable success criteria**\n",
    "1. Conversion among the contacted 12% (precision) clearly above the overall conversion rate.\n",
    "2. Clearly better than a simple rule a CRM manager can apply without a model (contact the most recent buyers).\n",
    "3. The result holds in each of the campaigns, not only on average.\n",
    "4. Ideally: the campaign creates *extra* sales (uplift) — to be checked whether the data allows this.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Critical assessment of assumptions**\n",
    "- *Dataset facts* come only from the CSV. *Scenario assumptions*: 12% capacity, channel/discount fixed before targeting. *Analytical choices*: metric, baselines, model.\n",
    "- The margin per order is not given, so we do not invent profit; we show a **break-even margin** instead.\n",
    "- Predicting *who buys* is not the same as *who buys because of the campaign*. Loyal, recent buyers may buy anyway."
   ]
  },
