  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "739d9bf6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Evidence-based findings**\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Evidence:** converters last ordered 120 days ago (median) vs 189 days for non-converters; conversion falls from 8.1% (0–90 days) to 2.3% (271–365 days). **Interpretation:** recent buyers respond more often — recency is the main targeting signal. **Caution:** this does not mean the campaign *caused* their purchase; recent buyers may buy anyway.\n",
    "2. **Evidence:** previous orders are slightly higher for converters (5.31 vs 4.91), basket value is almost the same. **Interpretation:** frequency adds a little, basket value hardly. **Caution:** small differences can disappear in a model.\n",
    "3. **Evidence:** channel (4.3–6.0%), tier (4.6–6.3%) and campaign (4.9–5.7%) differ only a little, and the raw spellings split groups (e.g. Gold 6.3% vs gold 6.0%). **Interpretation:** categories must be cleaned before they can be compared. **Caution:** small differences on ~50 converters per group may be noise.\n",
    "\n",
    "**How this refines Phase 1:** because one variable (recency) carries most of the signal, a **simple recency rule** is a serious baseline. A model is only worth it if it beats that rule."
   ]
  },
