   "source": [
    "| Element | Design |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **User** | CRM / loyalty campaign manager; an analyst runs the notebook |\n",
    "| **When produced** | Once per campaign, after channel and discount are chosen, before sending. Customer data frozen on that date |\n",
    "| **Output** | Ranked list of loyalty members with a probability; top 12% flagged. Recency rule shown next to it as a fallback and sanity check |\n",
    "| **Action supported** | *Who* receives the campaign. **Not** which discount or channel (no evidence that these change conversion) |\n",
    "| **Pilot design** | Randomly hold back 20% of the top 12% (not contacted) and contact a random 2% of the remaining customers. Uplift = conversion contacted − conversion held back |\n",
    "| **Human oversight / override** | Manager may remove customers (complaints, opt-outs, contacted recently) or add customers; every override is logged with a reason |\n",
    "| **Monitoring (per campaign)** | Precision in top 12% vs base rate and vs recency rule; uplift from the hold-back group; cost per *extra* order; share of Bronze/Silver/Gold in the list; opt-out rate |\n",
    "| **Feedback / refresh trigger** | Retrain after every 2 campaigns with the new outcomes, or earlier if the base conversion rate moves more than 2 percentage points or a new channel/discount appears |\n",
    "| **Pause / stop condition** | Pause if precision in the top 12% falls to the base rate, if the model no longer beats the recency rule for two campaigns, or if the hold-back group converts as often as the contacted group (no uplift) |\n",
    "\n",
    "**Why this design.** The hold-back group creates the control group the current data is missing, so the next iteration can answer the real business question (*does the campaign cause extra purchases?*) instead of only *who buys*. Monitoring the tier mix makes sure the list does not slowly turn into \"Gold members only\"."
   ]
