### Personal Reflection 
Anastasia Kaplun

This assignment helped me understand how data science supports business decisions. The aim was not simply to predict which customers would purchase, but to select customers while staying within a 12% contact limit.

One of my main discoveries was the importance of data preparation. Inconsistent channel names, discounts recorded in different formats, and duplicate records can all affect the results. I also learned why post-campaign clicks and revenue should be excluded from the predictors: this information is not available when the contact decision is made.

The evaluation helped me understand the limitations of accuracy. Since most customers did not purchase, predicting no conversions could achieve high accuracy without helping the campaign manager. Precision and recall at the contact limit were more relevant measures. Comparing logistic regression with a simple recency rule also helped me assess whether the model added enough value to justify its complexity.

The key lesson for me was the distinction between predicting conversions and evaluating campaign effectiveness. A customer might purchase a product even without receiving an offer. Therefore, the model’s results do not prove that the campaign generates additional sales.

In future work, I would improve the validation process by fitting preprocessing within each training fold and evaluating performance on a new campaign. I would also investigate missing values more carefully rather than assuming that removing a small number of observations cannot introduce bias. Overall, this assignment taught me to connect analytical results to business needs, question assumptions, and acknowledge limitations before making a recommendation.
